the Hulk may be one of Marvel's most  famous heroes but after the release of  The Incredible Hulk Bruce Banner was  sidelined until the Avengers rolled out  around four years later and even then  the Hulk's role in the MCU has largely  been a supporting one so why did we  never get to see the Incredible Hulk -  when the MCU began their core actors  were in place Chris Evans is Captain  America Chris Hemsworth playing Thor and  of course Robert Downey jr. nailing the  role of Iron Man well three out of four  isn't bad because we all know what  happened with Bruce Banner so why did  Edward Norton fail to make the cut for  Earth's Mightiest Heroes while most of  the actors playing Avengers have had a  long and prosperous relationship with  Marvel Studios Norton seems to have  gotten off on the wrong foot from day  one in fact he doesn't appear to have  even been the first choice for the role  in an interview with The Huffington Post  director Louis Leterrier let it slip  that while he appreciated what Norton  brought to the table his first pick had  actually been Mark Ruffalo I'm to fill  you on top of his runner-up status  numerous rumors have arisen over the  years suggesting that Norton may have  been difficult to work with on the set  that he was very vocal at times when he  disagreed with something and that he was  overly involved in tampering with the  script while none of these are  particularly rare qualities and  Hollywood stars they're not likely to  stack the deck in your favor either it  didn't take long after the film came out  for the first hints of discord to begin  surfacing two years after the incredible  Hulk's release Marvel boss Kevin Feige  made the announcement that Norton would  not be brought back to portray the title  role of Bruce Banner in the Avengers at  the time Feige insisted that the  decision wasn't a monetary one his  statement read our decision is  definitely not based on monetary factors  but instead rooted in the need for an  actor who embodies the creativity and  collaborative spirits of our other  talented cast members Feige statement  made it clear that something had been  going on behind closed doors something  more than a few friendly disagreements  over the script  luckily the Hulk was still set to appear  in the first Avengers movie and would  continue to make appearances in other  characters future films but the removal  of Norton from the role shed a little  light on why Ironman played by the  immensely popular Robert Downey jr.  became the center  burgeoning franchise while the Hulk  languished on the sidelines Norton's  agent was quick to respond to Feige  statement and his rebuttal he said this  offensive statement from Kevin Feige at  Marvel is a purposefully misleading  inappropriate attempt to paint our  clients in a negative light the actor  himself took to social media not to  attack Marvel but rather to express his  disappointment at the way things had  turned out he wrote I sincerely hoped it  could happen and be great for everyone  but it hasn't turned out as we all poked  I know this is disappointing to many  people and that makes me sad but I am  very sincerely grateful to Marvel for  extending the offer and even more for  giving me the chance to be a part of the  Hulk's long and excellent history these  responses from Norton and his agent  seemed to indicate that no matter how  bad things might have been behind the  scenes Norton had continued to hope that  he would reprise his role unfortunately  by Gea's decision put an end to those  hopes as the dust settled Norton  eventually traded his regretful tune for  one of indifference explaining a few  years later that he had lost interest in  the role and instead wanted to act in  other movies and genres while it's been  rumored for years that Norton isn't the  easiest person to work with that doesn't  mean the blame for his removal deserves  to fall squarely on his hulked out  shoulders both Marvel Studios and Disney  have developed a track record over the  years for dismissing various personnel  who haven't fallen in line with their  vision for example Thor the dark worlds  first director patty Jenkins said she  didn't think she could make a great film  out of their script rather than allowing  her to adjust the story the studio  parted ways with the director who went  on to head up Wonder Woman while Marvel  ended up with a subpar sequel to their  Thor franchise Terrence Howard also  infamously lost the role of James Rhodey  Rhodes to Don Cheadle after iron man and  of course there's guardians of the  galaxy franchise mastermind James Gunn  who is fired in 2018 after Internet  users dug up some of his old and quite  offensive tweets while Disney may have  ultimately reinstated gun to his  previous position the whole Norton  debacle wasn't the only  behind-the-scenes misstep Marvel Studios  hasn't Dewart although the actual  details of the behind the scenes breakup  may never be cleared up  one thing was crystal clear as the door  swung shot on Norton  the Hulk's MCU storyline wasn't going  anywhere fast rights are a tricky  business in Hollywood in the case of a  standalone Hulk sequel though there are  even more nuanced than usual while Sony  may own the rights to spider-man  for example the rights to Hulk are more  or less split between Marvel and  Universal but unlike the innovative time  sharing agreement between Sony and  Marvel for Spidey  the Hulk situation has been complicated  by the less than stellar relationship  between the two studios holding the  strings according to forbes Marvel  regained the rights to the Hulk  character in 2005 when Universal  neglected to purchase a sequel to  angley's 2003 movie Hulk but while that  meant Marvel was given the chance to  integrate Hulk into their Cinematic  Universe Universal retained one critical  piece of the puzzle the rights of first  refusal over the distribution of any  solo Hulk film in other words while  Marvel can use the Hulk character if  they decide to make Hulk movie Universal  gets first dibs on distributing the  feature of course Marvel has used other  studios to distribute their movies  before and the lack of distribution  rights alone isn't enough of a reason to  put the kibosh on a sequel but  considering all the other issues the  Incredible Hulk faced during production  and after its release and can't have  helped things much either it's easy to  look at the quarter of a billion that  the Incredible Hulk made in theaters and  dismiss money as a motivation in  Marvel's hesitation to produce a sequel  but the numbers are actually a bit of an  issue even though the movie was released  before the days when Marvel was pumping  out billion dollar projects a couple of  times a year the movie still  underperformed at the box office during  its run norton's Hulk brought in 263  million four hundred and twenty seven  thousand five hundred and fifty one  dollars which is a respectable sum to  say the least but take Iron Man for  comparison that movie which was released  just a few months prior to the  Incredible Hulk and which costs 10  million dollars less to make brought in  twice as much in global ticket sales and  while the Incredible Hulk may not have  been a complete flop it's less than  stellar box-office performance and  middling critical reception likely  suggested to Marvel that the Hulk alone  just isn't compelling in  to draw a big crowd having the Hulk show  up for a handful of epic fight scenes is  exactly the kind of integration that  makes its presence in Marvel's more  recent films worthwhile but the truth is  it costs a lot to produce a whole film  centered on a completely CGI character  and that goes doubly when any scene  therein is going to have to include a  kitchen sinks worth of flying cars  crumbling buildings and all kinds of  mayhem while a huge investment in  special-effects is part and parcel of  bigger crossover releases like The  Avengers movies an entire film of that  nature focused on a single character is  a daunting task to expect the studio to  take on and when you take into  consideration the disappointing box  office reception of the Incredible Hulk  it becomes that much easier to believe  that the Marvel and studio execs  involved in the decision would hesitate  before signing off on such an expensive  project  although the snowball effect of subpar  ticket sales uninspired reviews  production costs behind the scenes drama  and distribution rights would have  already been more than enough to put the  final nail in the coffin at any Hulk  sequel that's not the end of the story  in many ways the true Achilles heel of  the Hulk's one and only MCU outing was  that it simply failed to fit the tone of  the wider MCU whether it was the solemn  introspection that Norton tried to bring  to the role or the inherent difficulties  in exploring the Hulk's mindless  destruction as the centrepiece of a  story The Incredible Hulk ended up  struggling to hit the effortlessly  entertaining tone of most of the  following MCU films taking place in  phase 1 of the MCU Polk's film was set  against characters like the selfishly  sarcastic Tony Stark the lovably loyal  Steve Rogers and the hilariously hunky  Thor all of whom were busy setting the  stage for the first Avengers movie in  comparison Hulk's more serious overtones  stood out like a big green sore thumb  and made the film dark enough to dampen  the enthusiasm of even the most ardent  Marvel fan as if the sobering tone of  the film wasn't enough there's also the  fact that the story started by dropping  the audience into a random spot in  Banner and the Hulk story arc and then  proceeded to take it absolutely nowhere  after skipping over the famous gamma  radiation exposure incident the film  proceeded to meander awkwardly through  banners harrowing Leone her  adventures he finally flashed a little  superhero muscle when he subdued  abomination in the streets of Harlem but  when the film ended shortly after things  once again took a step backward banners  take away from the experience was simply  that he had found a way to come to terms  with the other guy and while this  appeared to be a positive step forward  for the character the fact that it was  quickly retcon when Ruffalo's distressed  banner was brought back on the scene and  the avengers made the whole point of the  film pretty much pointless in july 2017  Mark Ruffalo predicted that quote a  standalone Hulk movie will never happen  and while that may have seemed like the  end of the story you could argue that a  secret Incredible Hulk follow up already  exists  [Applause]  around the same time that Ruffalo dashed  fans hopes have ever seen another solo  Hulk film the actor also revealed in  another interview that he sat down with  Kevin Feige and the two had brainstormed  what a standalone film for the Hulk  would look like once they had a good  idea to work from they decided to  implement it into the wider MCU by  divvying up the story across three  separate movies cleverly none of these  would be released as a standalone film  thus sidestepping the rights issue with  Universal instead the Hulk storyline  would be woven through thor ragnarok  Avengers infinity war and Avengers  endgame during which his sequel  storyline could finally be told one  piece at a time  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

